I have a R shiny webApp, that can import raster layers, analyze them and plotting the results in four different output types (raster-plot, text, graph-plot). Instead of plotting the results, I would like to "send/export" them as files (*.geotiff, *.txt or similar) to be used in a different WebApp (not R shiny). They should behave like outputs plotted within R shiny (reactivity: outputs should change every time, the inputs are changed; some sort of sessionID: multiple users should be able to create different outputs at the same time).

Unfortunately, I have very little knowledge about HTML/CSS/JavaScript and the Building Outputs <http://rstudio.github.io/shiny/tutorial/#building-outputs> chapter of the otherwise great R Shiny Tutorial is kind of confusing to me.

Can someone post an easy example of how such an output function would look like and how the output can be used in other webapps(HTML/CSS/JavaScript)? Here is some example code:

server.R
# Define server logic for random distribution application
shinyServer(function(input, output) {

  data <- reactive({
    inFile <- input$example_layer #data import

    if (is.null(inFile))
      return(NULL)

    raster.file<- raster(inFile$datapath) #transform to raster

  })

  output$raster.plot <- renderPlot({

    plot(data()) #plot raster data

  })

  #create histogram displaying frquency of values in raster dataset
  output$hist.plot <- renderPlot({

    values_test<-getValues(stack(data()))
    hist(values_test)

  })

  # Generate a summary of the data
  output$summary <- renderPrint({
    summary(data()) # summary of the raster data
  })
})
ui.R:
library(shiny)

shinyUI(pageWithSidebar(

  # Application title
  headerPanel("test"),

  sidebarPanel(

    #Import new raster Layer
    fileInput('example_layer', 'Choose Raster Layer (ASCII)', multiple=FALSE, accept='asc')

  ),

  # Show a tabset that includes a plot, histogram and summary
  mainPanel(
    tabsetPanel(
      tabPanel("Raster-Plot", plotOutput("raster.plot")),
      tabPanel("Histogram", plotOutput("hist.plot")),
      tabPanel("Summary", verbatimTextOutput("summary"))

    )
  )
))
Thank you very much!

