taking bits from my apps, something like this should work:

# ui.R  (say in a sidebarPanel or mainPanel or any wellPanel)

      list(downloadButton('downloadEPS', "Save ps"),downloadButton('downloadPDF', "pdf"))

# server.R

    output$downloadEPS <- downloadHandler(
    filename = "plot.ps <http://plot.ps>", content = function(file) {
      postscript(file, height=6, width=8, onefile = FALSE, horizontal = FALSE)
      print(Plot())
      dev.off()
    })

    output$downloadPDF <- downloadHandler(
    filename = "plot.pdf", content = function(file) {
      pdf(file, height=6, width=8)
      print(Plot())
      dev.off()
    })

If you're concerned about the file name, something like this could be adapted (the commented-out piece saves a cvs file, the non-commented out saves an .RData file): the point is to show that 1) you can use an observer instead of a downloadHandler, depending on how you plan the user to save things, 2) to show one way in which you can make approximately unique filenames (if that's not good enough, you can add a random string to the filename).

    # Save all answers after click on "submit"
    observe({
      if(is.null(input$submit) || input$submit == 0) {return()}
#      filename <- paste0("answers-",input$courseName, "-", input$userName, "-", as.numeric(Sys.time()), ".csv")
#      write.table(values$A, 'file' = filename, 'sep' = ",", 'col.names' = FALSE)
      filename <- paste0("/data/answers-",input$courseName, "-", input$userName, "-", as.numeric(Sys.time()), ".RData")
      save(values$A, file = filename, compress = "xz")
    })

many ways you can action this, but a simple one is an actionButton (with inputId="submit", above)

